import hsrawprgm()


module(Python)
  # # Reading Python
    An importer: a way of reading files written in another syntax, declared in Catln rather than
    built into the compiler. A file which writes `import "python/importer.ct"` on one line may write
    `import python("thing.py")` on the next, and the compiler will read that file by building this
    page and running what is declared below.
    The reading itself is still the compiler's, behind [#runtime]. A Python file is read with
    TreeSitter, which is not something to rewrite in Catln for the sake of saying it was — what has
    moved is where the importer is DECLARED, from a table in the compiler to a page a program can
    import. The same Haskell reads the file either way: [Syntax.Python.Parser.pyParseFile] is called
    both by the syntax the compiler still registers and by the primitive below.
    This page writes `import hsrawprgm()`, because that is where [/Catln/ImportResult] and the raw
    program inside it are declared. No page of @stack/core/@ may write that import — see
    @core/hsprgm.ct@, which says why — and this page is not one of them.
  parsePython(path -> String) -> /Catln/ImportResult
    # [parsePython] reads the Python module at [path] as the program it declares: its functions and
      classes as definitions, its own imports dropped, and @python/builtins.ct@ imported for the
      built-ins its expressions are rewritten into.
    #importer(name= "python", extensions= "py")
    #runtime("pyImport")
